WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:05.120
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:07.080
None of these faces are real.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:13.170
And today we are going to find out whether
these synthetic humans can, in a way, pass

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:17.610
for real humans, but not in the sense that
you might think.

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:22.520
Now, through the power of computer graphics
algorithms, we are able to create virtual

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:27.310
worlds, and of course, within those virtual
worlds, virtual humans too.

00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:30.260
So, here is a wacky idea.

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:36.140
If we have all this virtual data, why not
use these instead of real photos to train

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:38.149
new neural networks?

00:00:38.149 --> 00:00:40.409
Hmm…wait a second.

00:00:40.409 --> 00:00:44.010
Maybe this idea is not so wacky after all.

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:48.889
Especially because we can generate as many
of these virtual humans as we wish, and all

00:00:48.889 --> 00:00:51.649
this data is perfectly annotated.

00:00:51.649 --> 00:00:56.850
The location and shape of the eyebrows is
known, even when it they are occluded, and

00:00:56.850 --> 00:01:02.100
we know the depth and geometry of every single
hair strand of the beard.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:07.520
If done well, there will be no issues about
the identity of the subjects, or the distribution

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:08.570
of the data.

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:13.619
Also, we are not limited by our wardrobe or
the environments we have access to.

00:01:13.619 --> 00:01:17.020
In this virtual world, we can do anything
we wish.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:18.100
So good!

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:23.539
But of course, here is the ultimate question
that decides the fate of this project.

00:01:23.539 --> 00:01:26.159
And that question is: does this work?

00:01:26.159 --> 00:01:31.659
Now, we can give all this data to train a
neural network, and the crazy thing about

00:01:31.659 --> 00:01:36.310
this is that this neural network never saw
a real human.

00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:39.079
So here’s the ultimate test.

00:01:39.079 --> 00:01:41.159
Videos of real humans.

00:01:41.159 --> 00:01:47.070
Now, hold on to your papers and and, let’s
see if the little AI can label the image and

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:49.580
find the important landmarks.

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:50.580
Wow.

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:56.000
I can hardly believe my eyes - it not only
does it for a still image, but for even a

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:02.299
video, and it is so accurate from frame to
frame that no flickering artifacts emerge.

00:02:02.299 --> 00:02:04.490
That is outstanding.

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:09.819
And get this, the measurements say that it
can stand up to other state of the art detector

00:02:09.819 --> 00:02:13.870
neural networks that were trained on real
human faces.

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:16.040
And…oh my!

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:18.709
Are you seeing what I am seeing?

00:02:18.709 --> 00:02:20.319
Can this really be?

00:02:20.319 --> 00:02:25.370
If we use the same neural network to learn
on real human faces, it actually won’t to

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:33.230
better at all, in fact, it will do worse than
the same AI with the virtual data.

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:34.280
That is insanity.

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:40.730
The advantages of this practically infinitely
flexible synthetic dataset shows really well

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:41.730
here.

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:46.849
The paper also discusses in detail that that
this only holds if we really use the synthetic

00:02:46.849 --> 00:02:53.450
dataset well and include different rotations
and lighting environments for the same photos.

00:02:53.450 --> 00:02:57.670
Something that is not always so easy in real
environments.

00:02:57.670 --> 00:03:02.379
Now this test was called face parsing, and
now comes landmark detection.

00:03:02.379 --> 00:03:07.260
This also works remarkably well, but wait
a second.

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:10.440
Once again, are you seeing what I am seeing?

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:15.849
The landmarks can rotate all they want, and
it will know where they should be, even if

00:03:15.849 --> 00:03:21.629
they are occluded by headphones, the hair,
or when they are not visible at all.

00:03:21.629 --> 00:03:24.780
Now, of course, not even this technique is
perfect.

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:32.070
Tracking the eyes correctly requires additional
considerations and real data, but only for

00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:35.430
that, which is relatively easy to produce.

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:40.980
Also, the simulator at this point can only
generate the head and the neck regions, and

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:41.980
nothing else.

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:47.189
But, you know the drill, a couple papers down
the line, and I am sure that this will be

00:03:47.189 --> 00:03:51.670
able to generate full human bodies.

00:03:51.670 --> 00:04:07.390
Thanks for watching and for 

00:04:07.390 --> 00:04:42.090
your generous support, and I'll see you next
time!

